title: LSP server should notice its binary was replaced (self-update/new install) and restart itself
updated_at: "2026-08-31T10:18:14Z"
---
## Expected Behavior

A long-running `openplanet-lsp` in LSP mode notices when the executable
it was launched from has been replaced on disk (self-update, package
manager, manual install) and restarts so the editor gets the new version
without a manual reload. Diagnostics stop being served by a stale binary
hours after an upgrade.

## Actual Behavior

Nothing watches the binary. After an update the old process keeps
serving until the editor restarts it by chance. (Same stale-server shape
as the GH #18 motivating incident, where answers came from a process
older than what was on disk.)

## Design decisions to settle (discuss before/while implementing)

- **Detection:** poll `std::env::current_exe()` metadata (mtime/len, and
  inode on unix) on a slow timer (~30s) vs filesystem watch. Polling is
  probably enough and avoids watcher platform quirks. Windows caveat: a
  running exe is replaced by rename-swap, so compare by PATH metadata,
  not by open-file identity; on unix the path may point at a NEW inode
  while /proc/self/exe still holds the old one — that difference IS the
  signal.
- **Restart mechanism:** LSP has no server-initiated restart. Options:
  (a) exit cleanly after finishing in-flight work — VS Code's
  LanguageClient auto-respawns an exited server (bounded retries);
  (b) custom notification to our extension (../lsp-openplanet-ext) so it
  restarts the client deliberately — more control, needs ext work;
  (c) exec-replace ourselves (unix only, loses Windows).
  Rec: (b) as primary with (a) as fallback when the custom capability
  isn't acknowledged by the client.
- **Grace:** only restart when idle (no in-flight requests, no dirty
  overlay state that would be lost); log the old→new version stamps.
- **Loop safety:** if the replacement binary dies immediately, do not
  ping-pong — the extension side should back off (VS Code's built-in
  crash counter may already cover this).
- **Scope:** LSP mode only. `check`/TUI runs are short-lived; `--watch`
  TUI could get the same treatment later but is not this ticket.

## Test cases

- Metadata-change detection unit-testable behind a seam (fake exe path +
  temp file swap) — B032-style: no HOME/env mutation in-process.
- Replaced binary + idle server → restart signal emitted exactly once.
- Replaced binary + in-flight request → signal deferred until idle.
- Unchanged binary → no signal over many polls (negative control).
- Related plumbing: src/update.rs (self-update already knows how the
  swap is performed — reuse its knowledge of the swap shape rather than
  guessing).